# Recent advances in hollow metal-organic frameworks and their composites for heterogeneous thermal catalysis

1CAS Key Laboratory of Nanosystem and Hierarchical Fabrication, CAS Center for Excellence in Nanoscience, National Center for Nanoscience and Technology, Beijing 100190, China; 2College of Chemical Engineering, Qingdao University of Science and Technology, Qingdao 266042, China; 3School of Nanoscience and Technology, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China
eceived July 4, 2021; accepted August 15, 2021; published online October 8, 202
Hollow metal-organic frameworks (HoMOFs) as emerging materials have been arousing great interest in heterogeneous catalysis with respect to the solid counterparts, mainly because of their unique and intriguing features such as high surface to volume ratio, large void space, short channel length, and more exposed active sites. To achieve diverse catalysis, it is of great importance to combine HoMOFs with other active components for constructing smart composites. In this review, the recent advances in HoMOFs and their composites for heterogeneous catalysis are summarized. Firstly, the synthetic strategies for various HoMOFs with single shell, double shells or multiple shells are summarized, while their composites with yolk-shell, hollow sandwich and hollow satellite structures are described. Secondly, various catalytic reactions over HoMOFs and their composites are discussed, and moreover, the relationships among the active components, structures and their performances are illustrated. Thirdly, the potential challenges and future development on HoMOFs-based nanocatalysts are proposed. This review will bring some insights for better design and fabrication of heterogeneous nanocatalysts with the well-defined hollow structures.
controllable synthesis, hollow metal-organic framework, metal nanoparticles, hollow nanocomposite, heterogeneous catalysis
Citation: Wang H, Zheng F, Xue G, Wang Y, Li G, Tang Z. Recent advances in hollow metal-organic frameworks and their composites for heterogeneous thermal catalysis. Sc China Chem, 2021, 64: 1854–1874, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-021-1095-y
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also called porous coordination polymers [1], which are assembled by organic ligands and inorganic metal ions or clusters, have been arousing great attention in heterogeneous catalysis, because of their unique and intriguing features including the welldefined structures, large surface area, tunable pore size, diverse uncoordinated metal nodes and abundant organic ligands [2,3]. The porous coordination polymer assembled by $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ions and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine was first applied for catalytic aldehyde cyanosilylation in 1994 by Fujita’s group [4]. It is interesting that with the increase in aldehyde size, the yields of target products decreased dramatically, and they were $77\%$ for benzaldehyde, $40\%$ for 2-tolualdehyde, and $19\%$ for 3-tolualdehyde, exhibiting the molecular sieving effect of pore structures. During the past twenty years, various MOFs have been developed such as MIL series (MIL stands for Material Institut Lavoisier) [5], UiO series (UiO stands for University of Oslo) [6] and ZIF series (ZIF stands for zeolitic imidazolate frameworks) [7]. Until now over 20,000 kinds of MOFs have been successfully synthesized [1], and some of them are widely applied in heterogeneous catalytic reactions such as hydrogenation [8], oxidation [9], $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition [10] and Knoevenagel condensation [11]. Their catalytic performances strongly correlate with individual components of MOFs such as metal nodes, organic linkers, pore structures, and/or the synergistic functions among them. In addition, different active components such as Au [12], Pt [8] and Pd [6] are often combined with MOFs to construct the composites with diverse structures such as core-shell, sandwich, satellite structures and more complex structures for realizing different catalytic applications, because they could not only integrate the function of individual component, but also generate some novel and intriguing properties among them [13,14].
To achieve high catalytic performances, it is of great importance to control the morphology and size of MOFs, and even miniaturize them to the nanoscale, because the bulk counterparts present some shortcomings in the catalytic applications such as slow mass transfer and inaccessibility of internal space. Among various nanostructures, HoMOFs have been proven to be an emerging platform for enhancing their inherent properties owing to their high surface to volume ratio, large void space, short channel length, and more exposed active sites with respect to the bulk MOFs (Figure 1), and moreover, the related publications about the HoMOFs and their citations are continuously growing every year (Figure 2). In addition, HoMOFs based derivatives including hollow carbon materials [2,15], hollow metal oxides [16] and hollow metal hydroxides [17], have also being aroused great interest in constructing hierarchical functional nanocatalysts for electrocatalysis [18,19] and photocatalysis [20]. As contrast, HoMOFs have the unique advantages in heterogeneous catalysis such as the porous outer shell, abundant composition, the hybrid organic-inorganic properties and tunable open channels. Further, the uniform pores/ cavities of HoMOFs provide the diverse ways as well as confinement effects to host various components, and the HoMOFs could influence the intrinsic properties of secondary components and further adjust the catalytic performances.
Until now, many efforts have been dedicated to the rational preparation of HoMOFs as well as their composites with other active components for heterogeneous catalysis [2,14]. Although the advances regarding the fabrication of HoMOFs are realized, the further fabrication of diverse HoMOFs as well as the investigation of their catalytic properties, especially in organic transformations is still in its infancy but of potentially high interest. In this review, we give a brief summary for synthesis of HoMOFs and their composites as well as their applications in heterogeneous catalysis. Firstly, synthesis methods for various HoMOFs with single-shelled, double-shelled and multi-shelled structures, and even their composites including yolk-shell, hollow sandwich and hollow satellite structures are summarized. Then, the catalytic performances of HoMOFs based nanocatalysts are discussed, and the relationships among individual components, structures and their performances are illustrated. Finally, the challenges and future outlook about the synthesis of different HoMOFs and their composites for heterogeneous catalysis are prospected.
## 2 Synthesis of HoMOFs and their composites
Since the first HoMOF was reported by Wang et al. [21] in 2010, many progressive advances have been made in this emerging field. Until now dozens of HoMOFs have been developed with different morphologies [22] including singleshelled spheres/tubes/boxes [23–28], and hierarchically hollow structures [29–33]. Furthermore, novel techniques like machine-learning have been applied to control the size and shell thickness of HoMOFs [34]. To better comprehend the fast-developing field, it is of great significance to summarize the synthesis methods of different pristine HoMOFs. In this part, we will clarify the typical synthesis methods into three categories including hard-templating, soft-templating and template-free synthesis methods.
## 2.1 Synthesis of single-shelled HoMOFs
## 2.1.1 Hard-templating synthesis methods
Hard-templating strategy is widely applied to construct various HoMOFs [35]. Typically, the synthesis procedure includes three steps: synthesis of hard templates with different morphologies, nucleation and growth of MOF shells on the surface of hard templates, and removal of hard templates and formation of HoMOFs [36]. To date, different hard templates have been prepared, such as polystyrene (PS) [11,23,37], $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ [9,38], $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [12,39], and HoMOFs with different morphologies have been realized, such as spheres [24], octahedrons [40] and tubes [27]. The key issue involved is to make the heterogeneous nucleation and growth of MOFs around the hard templates. Also, the morphology and crystal structures of HoMOFs strongly correlate with the etching processes, because different polar and corrosive solvents are often utilized to etch the hard templates, and thus, the local structures of HoMOFs are often destroyed.
Polystyrene (PS), which is easily modified with different functional groups, could serve as the excellent robust substrate to hold the growth of MOF shells [22,37]. Moreover, the PS templates could be removed by organic solutions like $N\mathrm{,}N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), and dichloromethane. The first synthesis of hollow ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ was reported by Oh and co-workers [23] via using carboxylate-terminated PS spheres as hard template. In a typical synthesis, carboxylate-terminated PS spheres with a diameter of $0.87\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were added into a methanol solution containing 2-methylimidazole (2- MeIM) and $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}}}$ , and then, heated at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to get the core-shell $\mathrm{PS}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ microspheres by the stepwise solvothermal reaction. It is noted that carboxylate groups on the surfaces of PS spheres first interacted with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and initiated the growth of ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ . The as-prepared core-shell $\mathrm{PS}@\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{IF}{-}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ microspheres were then immersed into DMF to remove the PS cores and induce the formation of hollow ZIF-8 $\mathbf{\nabla}Z\mathbf{n})$ microspheres. The shell thickness could be adjusted by controlling the growth cycle of ZIF-8(Zn), such as too thin for one cycle, $50\mathrm{nm}$ for two cycles and $100\mathrm{nm}$ for three cycles (Figure 3(a)).
Similarly, hollow MIL-100(Fe) and HKUST- ${\mathsf{I}}({\mathsf{C u}})$ were successfully synthesized via using PS spheres as hard template [24]. The PS spheres were modified with sulfonyl group on their surfaces, and then, added into the ethanol solution containing $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions and stirred at room temperature. After that, the PS spheres were separated by centrifugation, and then, added into the ethanol solution containing 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ and stirred at room temperature to get the core-shell structure. After repeating the growth cycles for 90 times and removing PS spheres by DMF, hollow MIL-100(Fe) nanospheres with the diameter of $0.45\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and average shell thickness of $60\mathrm{nm}$ were synthesized successfully (Figure 3(b)). Also, hollow HKUST-1(Cu) nanospheres were synthesized by using ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions and $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ as the building blocks. The hollow HKUST$1(\mathrm{Cu})$ with average diameter of $1.4\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and shell thickness of $126\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained after 30 growth cycles (Figure 3(c)).
In addition, the synthesis of one-dimensional (1D) ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ nanotubes was reported by Qin and co-workers [28] via using the commercially available polycarbonate (PCTE) membranes as hard templates. First, the PCTE membranes with average pore size of $100\mathrm{nm}$ were floated on top of an aqueous solution of zinc acetate, $\mathrm{{Zn}(C H_{3}C O O)_{2}}$ . Then, the 1- octanol solution of 2-MeIM was added to make PCTE membranes locate on the interface of above two solutions. It is noted that the pore walls of membranes had a thin layer of poly( $N.$ -vinylpyrrolidinone) (PVP), which could anchor the initial nucleation and growth of ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ crystals. Since the surface diffusion rate of 2-MeIM is larger than inside the pores, solid rods form quickly at the top, which acts as a cap and prevents further 2-MeIM diffusion, and thus, leads to nanotube structures inside the pores. The hollow tubes with the average diameter of $100\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $40\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained.
Except for the organic polymers as hard templates, inorganic hard templates like $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [39], cobalt acetate hydroxide [41] and $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ [38] have been developed to fabricate different HMOFs. Yang and co-workers [12] reported a shape-controlled synthesis of hollow HKUST-1(Cu) by using $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ template, in which $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is used as the copper ion source for synthesis of HKUST- ${\mathsf{I}}({\mathsf{C u}})$ . In detail, PVP could control the growth rate and morphology of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ crystals including octahedron with an average edge length of $600\mathrm{nm}$ , truncate octahedron with the average diameter of $650\mathrm{nm}$ , and corner-truncated cube with the average size of $380\mathrm{nm}$ . The synthesis of core-shell $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}(\mathrm{Cu})$ was carried out by directly mixing ethanolic suspension of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ at room temperature without any other surfactants or modification pretreatments. Then, the obtained $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}(\mathrm{Cu})$ was immersed with a diluted HCl solution to etch the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ core. The HoMOFs with the average shell thickness of $210\mathrm{nm}$ were composed of building units of HKUST-1(Cu) octahedral nanoparticles (NPs). Moreover, the cavity shape of the hollow HKUST-1 $\mathrm{(Cu)}$ was highly dependent on the shape of the initial $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ cores (Figure 3(d)). Furthermore, Bae et al. [41] reported the synthesis of hollow MOF-74(Co) rods via using the cobalt acetate hydroxide as hard templates and metal precursors. First, Co nanorods of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{5}$ were synthesized via PVP-assisted method. The obtained Co nanorods have a diameter of $100\mathrm{nm}$ and a length of $500\mathrm{nm}$ . Then, Co nanorods were added into DMF solution containing 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}D O B D C)}$ and stirred at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After etching the Co nanorods with methanol, the hollow MOF-74(Co) rods characteristic with a diameter of $180\mathrm{nm}$ , a length of $500\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $40\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained (Figure 3(e)).
Due to the epitaxial growth of MOFs on the isostructural crystal surfaces, MOFs could be used as hard templates for synthesis of different HoMOFs, such as ZIF-67(Co) and ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ [40]. Fischer and co-workers [30] constructed the hollow ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ nanostructures by using ZIF-67(Co) as hard template. The as-prepared ZIF-67(Co) with an average size of $300\mathrm{nm}$ was dispersed in methanol solution containing 2-MeIM and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , and then ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ could epitaxially grow on the ZIF-67(Co) seed crystals, because of the approximate crystal structure of ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ and ZIF-67(Co). It is noted that the ZIF-67(Co) is less stable than ZIF-8(Zn), which could be removed by the solvothermal condition at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{{h}}$ . The obtained hollow ZIF-8 $\mathsf{\Omega}^{\prime}(Z\mathsf{n})$ structures are characteristic with the average diameter of $400\mathrm{nm}$ and average shell thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ . In addition, Tsung and coworkers [40] synthesized hollow UiO- $66(Z\mathrm{r})$ via using UiO$66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ as hard template. In detail, $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ was synthesized by reacting $\mathrm{zrCl}_{4}$ with $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ under solvothermal condition, and they were characteristic with the average diameter of $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . UiO- $66(Z\mathrm{r})$ could epitaxially grow on the surfaces of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ in the DMF solution containing ${Z\mathrm{r}}^{4+}$ ions and terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ . The DOBDC linkers in $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ cores could be oxidized and cleaved into small molecules in the presence of concentrated nitric acid. The obtained hollow UiO- $66(Z\mathrm{r})$ is characteristic with average diameter of $500\mathrm{nm}$ and average shell thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3(f)). Moreover, this method can be expended to synthesize other hollow UiO-66 $(Z\mathbf{r})$ with different functional groups such as $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , $-\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and –Br. Further, Tian and co-workers [42] developed hollow $\mathrm{PCN}{-}222(\mathrm{Zr})$ (PCN=porous coordination network) via using ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ as hard template. A prefabricated ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ with average diameter of $90\mathrm{nm}$ was gradually digested by the acidic precursor solution, and a shell of PCN-222 $(Z\mathrm{r})$ was formed via the coordination-driven self-assembly of the tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP) ligand and ${Z\mathrm{r}}^{4+}$ ions, affording hollow PCN-222 $\mathrm{\underline{{Zr}})}$ spheres with the cavity size of $90\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $24~\mathrm{nm}$ .
In a word, hard-templating methods are universal and effective to construct various HoMOFs. The morphology of HMOFs can be well controlled by exquisite modification of hard templates. However, there are still some challenges in hard-templating methods. The hard templates are limited to construct specific kinds of HoMOFs, and diverse hard templates should be further developed. Besides, the HoMOFs tend to collapse after removal of templates, and thus, the suitable hard templates and mild etching process should be developed for constructing the well-defined HoMOFs.
## 2.1.2 Soft-templating synthesis methods
Soft-templating method is also utilized to synthesize various hollow nanostructure [43–46]. To date, the typical soft templates have been developed to synthesize HoMOFs, such as micelles [33,47], liquid droplets [48–52] and gas bubbles [53]. The formation of HoMOFs mainly relies on the selfassembly of precursors at the template-solution interface through physical or chemical interactions. Soft templates could be removed under the mild condition and allow the better preservation of morphology and composition of HoMOFs with respect to hard templates.
The amphiphilic surfactants, which can interact with metal ions and organic linkers by electrostatic interaction and hydrogen bonds, are often applied to form micelle templates and fabricate the hollow structures [54]. For example, Zeng and co-workers [55] reported the synthesis of hollow ZIF-67 (Co) via using cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) cationic vesicles as soft templates. The synthesis process involves the mixing of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , 2-MeIM and CTAB in aqueous medium. $\mathrm{CTA}^{+}$ vesicles and the [Co-complex] $\left([\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{MeIM})_{2}(\mathrm{L}_{2})]^{-}\right.$ , L represents $\mathrm{MeIM}^{-}$ and auxiliary charged ligands) were first formed. Then, [Co-complex] and other anions were distributed around the $\mathrm{CTA}^{+}$ ammonium headgroups through electrostatic interaction to balance the overall charges. After that, [Co-complex] species concentrated on the surface of the vesicles acted as nucleation sites for the heterogeneous outward-growth of ZIF-67(Co) crystals. Lastly, hollow ZIF-67(Co) spheres with the average diameter of $325\mathrm{nm}$ and average shell thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained by using DMF and methanol as etching agents (Figure 4(a)). This strategy was also extended to fabricate hollow HKUST-1(Cu) spheres with an average diameter of $800\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $150\mathrm{nm}$ by adding triethylamine to deprotonate the relatively water-insoluble $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ ligands.
Ji and co-workers [33] reported the synthesis of hollow Mn-BTC via using PVP micelle template. In a typical synthesis, spherical PVP micelles were generated with the carbonyl groups, which could attribute to the formation of hydrogen bonds between abundant hydrophilic groups of PVP and alcohol solution. Then, the $\mathrm{Mn(II)}$ acetate and $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{3}B T C}$ were adsorbed onto the surface of the spherical micelles by the interaction of hydrogen bonds derived from their ample hydroxyl groups, and thus forming Mn-BTC nanocrystals. The growth of Mn-BTC was perpendicular to the surface of micelle spheres due to the fast growth rate, and PVP@Mn-BTC microspheres were formed, and the shells were assembled by numerous 1D Mn-BTC nanorods with an average diameter of $100\mathrm{nm}$ and length of $500\mathrm{nm}$ . After removing PVP with ethanol, the hollow Mn-BTC spheres with an average diameter of $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and shell thickness of $500\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained.
The metal-sodium deoxycholate (metal-NaDC) hydrogel was used as soft template for constructing hollow ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ nanotubes [56]. In detail, the NaDC with abundant hydroxyl and carboxyl groups could attract $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and selfaggregate to form hydrogel fibers at $\mathsf{p H}7$ . Then, 2-MeIM ligand was added into the solution, while the hydrogel fibers could provide $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions to grow ZIF-8(Zn) rods. The obtained products were separated and washed with $\mathsf{p H}$ buffer solution $(0.1\mathrm{M},\mathsf{p H}7.0)$ to remove the NaDC. The obtained ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ nanotubes are characteristic with an average diameter of $300\mathrm{nm}$ and average shell thickness of $30\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 4(b)). The morphology of hollow structures was further adjusted by using Burkholderia cepacia lipase (BCL). The carboxylate groups of BCL could interact with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and greatly influence the compactness and roughness of the NaDC hydrogel fiber. The hydrogel fibers gradually decomposed into nanospheres as soft templates to grow ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ . With the concentration of $4~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ BCL, hollow ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanospheres were obtained with the average diameter of $250\mathrm{nm}$ and average shell thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ .
Except for micelle templates, emulsion droplet template refers to one liquid finely dispersed across another liquid phase stabilized by surfactant molecules or small solid particles. Such resulting liquid-liquid interface could work as soft template to grow MOF shells [57]. Xu et al. [58] developed a water-in-oil emulsion using PVP as stabilizer and 1-octanol as the oil phase. Generally, the aqueous phase was prepared by dissolving $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions into deionize water containing PVP. The oil phase was prepared by dissolving 2-
MeIM into 1-octanol to form the stable solutions. Then, the aqueous solution was added into 1-octanol and stirred/vortexed to obtain a stable inverse emulsion. Next, the organicligand solution was added into the above emulsion and gently shaken to initiate interfacial reaction. In this process, the emulsion droplets act as the temporary spherical templates, which could be easily removed during the purification process without etching. The hollow ZIF-8(Zn) nanospheres with average diameters around $130\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained (Figure 4(c)), while the shell thickness could increase from 15 to $70\mathrm{nm}$ with increasing the reaction time from 1 to $48\mathrm{{h}}$ . Besides, Eddaoudi et al. [29] reported the synthesis and assembly of MOF cubes into hollow microspheres using Pickering emulsion. In detail, in the presence of polyoxyethylene (20), sorbitan trioleate (tween-85) and PVP, the emulsion droplet spheres were formed. Besides, the Fesoc-MOF nanocubes assembled at the surface of emulsion droplet spheres due to the surface driving force. The internal emulsion droplets could be removed by centrifugation and washing with ethanol, and hollow Fe-soc-MOF spheres could be obtained. The small hollow colloidosomes assembled from the uniform cubes with an edge length of $310\mathrm{nm}$ , and the inner cavity diameter was in the range of $3{-}5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Figure 4(d)). Furthermore, this approach was extended to synthesize the hollow Ga-soc-MOF by using Ga3+ ions instead of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions [51].
Similar with emulsions, the gas-liquid interface could serve as a soft template for the synthesis of the hollow porous structures. Zhang and co-workers [53] reported a gas-liquid interface route to prepare HoMOFs by injecting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ bubbles into the ionic liquid phase that solubilized surfactant $N.$ -ethyl perfluorooctylsulfonamide and the precursors of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ . The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ bubbles generated in ionic liquid can provide numerous gas-liquid interfaces, serving as the nucleation or agglomeration centers for the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC nanocrystals. After releasing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by depressurization, the hollow tetrahedron-like $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC with the average particle size of $3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and shell thickness of $300\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained.
In a word, soft-templating synthesis methods provide more choices to construct various HoMOFs. Suitable solvents and surfactants are the key points in the construction of different HoMOFs via using soft templates. However, surfactants are often hard to eliminate completely and might affect the further catalytic application. Besides, the well-controlled morphology and composition of HoMOFs are still big challenges via using soft templates due to the inherent difficulties in modulating the soft templates with different morphologies.
## 2.1.3 Template-free synthesis methods
To date, although various HoMOFs have been constructed using template-assisted strategies, template-free methods have also been arousing great attention in the synthesis of HoMOFs with different morphologies due to the high costefficiency, process simplicity, high product uniformity and method flexibility [59]. In this section, we will summarize the recent progress on the synthesis of versatile HoMOFs via using template-free methods, such as Ostwald ripening [21,60], competitive coordination [61,62], chemical etching [32,51,63–69] and ligand exchange [70–72].
(1) Ostwald ripening method
Ostwald ripening is a physical phenomenon that refers to the growth of small-sized crystals with a high solubility into the large ones [2]. It is noted that the internal crystallites, which are smaller and/or less compact than the outside, will resolve in the solution, and the larger outside crystallites continue to aggregate and grow up, thus forming the hollow polycrystalline structures [22].
The first synthesis of HoMOFs via Ostwald ripening was reported by Ma and co-workers [21]. Typically, the coordination polymer Fe-Fc microspheres composed of Fe3+ ions and $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -ferrocenedicarboxylic acid were obtained via the solvothermal method. By controlling the reaction time, the morphology transformation of MOFs occurs. At the reaction time of $8\mathrm{h}$ , only solid spheres with the average diameter of $5.18\upmu\mathrm{m}$ are achieved. With prolonging the reaction time to $24\mathrm{h}$ , the hollow spheres with the average diameter of $4.02\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and shell thickness of $600\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained (Figure 5(a)). To fully verify the formation of HoMOF microspheres, the aging of the solid spheres in pure DMF without initial reagents at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was performed. With the prolonged aging, no pronounced change in the size of microspheres was found, but the diameter of the inside cavities increased gradually. When aged for $6\mathrm{h}$ , the formation of the HoMOF microsphere was found, which mainly originated from the mass transfer of small crystallites inside the spheres to the outside owing to the occurrence of Ostwald ripening phenomenon. Similarly, Oh and co-workers [60] fabricated the hollow Zn-BTC spheres with the average size of $2.53\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and shell thickness of $350\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 5(b)). In a typical procedure, $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}}}$ and $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ were dissolved in DMF solution. After heating at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ , the formation of hollow $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC microparticles was observed. To investigate the formation of such an unusual hollow structure, the aliquots from the reaction vessel at different reaction times were monitored. At the reaction time of $15\mathrm{min}$ , the solid microspheres were formed. More importantly, the secondary growth of MOFs onto the surfaces of the initially formed solid Zn-BTC microspheres was found at $20\mathrm{min}$ . Interestingly, the dissolution of the initially formed solid $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC microspheres occurred during the secondary growth of MOFs, thus forming the hollow $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC microspheres.
Besides, the morphology transformation could be driven by surface energy [73–75] and microwave [76,77]. Wang et al. [25] reported the synthesis of hollow Zn/Ni-MOF nanocubes (Zn/Ni-MOF-2) by reacting $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}B D C}$ with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions in the mixed solvents containing $N\mathrm{,}N^{\prime}$ -dimethylacetamide (DMAC), ethanol and PVP. At the reaction time of $0.1\mathrm{~h~}$ , high-surface-energy concave nanocubes with a side length of $400\mathrm{nm}$ were observed, and then, a small amount of the flexible nanosheets was formed and adhered to the outer surfaces of the concave nanocubes in order to decrease the surface energy. At the reaction time of $1.5\mathrm{h}$ , the concave features became more distinct and a large amount of the nanosheets were assembled around the etched nanocubes. After $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ reaction, the hollow ${\mathrm{Zn/Ni/-MOF}}$ nanocubes with the average size of $500\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $100\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained (Figure 5(c)).
Obviously, using the Ostwald ripening method could synthesize the HoMOFs in a one-pot process, which strongly correlates with the reaction temperature, reaction time and concentration of precursors.
(2) Competitive coordination method
Competitive coordination methods are widely used to synthesize different HoMOFs via the introduction of monocarboxylate acid or some anions like POM to coordinate with the metal clusters over ditopic or polytopic organic linkers [61,62,78].
Wang et al. [62] reported the synthesis of uniform and monodisperse bowl-like MOF microcapsule (MBM) via a competitive coordination strategy, which involves a solvothermal reaction of cobalt(III) acetylacetonate $[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}]$ , $\mathrm{{H}}_{3}\mathrm{{BTC}}$ , hexaammonium molybdate $[\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{6}}\cdot$ $\mathrm{Mo}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{24}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]$ , hexamethylenetetramine (HMTA), thioacetamide (TAA), and PVP at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . Because of hostguest supramolecular interactions between metal ions and molybdate, molybdate anions could partially substitute BTC groups to form Co-POM-BTC supramolecules. The S atoms of TAA might participate simultaneously through the interaction between S and Co. As a result, the Co-BTC nanospheres were dissolved with molybdate anions and S to regrow an outer shell including Co, BTC and competitive reagents. Due to the outward ion diffusion, hollow bowl-like microcapsules were formed, which are characteristic of hollow cavities with a diameter of approximately $300\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of about $50\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 5(d, e)).
Besides, Wang et al. [61] synthesized the hollow zirconium-porphyritic MOF nanotubes via using benzoic acid, and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ as coordination modulators. In detail, the hollow nanotubes are synthesized by the solvothermal reaction of zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ , TCPP, benzoic acid, and trace $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ in DMF solution at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Incomplete nanotubes with low crystallinity began to appear at the reaction time of $2\mathrm{h}$ . With further increasing the reaction time up to $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , the uniform hollow nanotube was obtained. In contrast, in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the needle-shaped bulk crystals were found, while amorphous products appeared without benzoic acid. It is noted that benzoic acid and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ were indispensable for the construction of hollow structures. The modulator benzoic acid could competitively coordinate with the zirconium clusters over TCPP. Moreover, introducing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ would accelerate the hydrolysis of zirconium salt and facilitate the deprotonation of TCPP to promote the crystal nucleation but inhibit growth. Therefore, the balance between crystal nucleation and growth could be well-tuned by introducing trace $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and benzoic acid, thus leading to the formation of hollow nanotubes with average diameter of $200\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $70\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 5(f)).
In short, the competitive coordination method is facile to construct delicate HoMOFs in a one-pot process, and the development of competitive coordination agents for the synthesis of HoMOFs is a vital part of the future work.
## (3) Selective etching method
Selective etching method is applied to synthesize different HoMOFs due to the inhomogeneous nature in solubility and chemical stability between the internal and external regions of the MOF crystals [79]. For example, Maspoch et al. [63] reported the reshaping of ZIFs into morphologies, including the cubic and tetrahedral crystals and even hollow boxes, by adjusting the $\mathsf{p H}$ value of xylenol orange $(\mathrm{XO})/\mathrm{HCl}$ etchant solution. XO could act as the surface protecting agent that coordinates with the liberated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions to form water-soluble complexes and make the etching process occur preferentially in the specific crystallographic directions. To synthesize the hollow boxes, a colloidal solution of rhombic dodecahedral ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ or ZIF-67(Co) crystals was mixed with XO/HCl etchant solution $\mathrm{(pH}{\sim}2.5)\$ . It is noted that the rhombic dodecahedral crystals were preferentially etched from (211) edges, and the etching solution was mainly digged the pinhole at the specific corner. After the chemical etching process, the hollow ZIF-8(Zn) and ZIF-67(Co) boxes are characteristic with four openings and thin shell thickness of $40\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 6(a)). In addition, a synergistic strategy for both protecting surface and selectively etching the internal part of MOFs was also developed to synthesize hollow ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ [65]. In detail, gallic acid (GA) or tannic acid (TA) was used as the etching acid because the acid molecules with large size and weak acidity could block the exposed surface of MOFs and release $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ions to etch the central positions of MOFs. After etching with TA, the hollow ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ with the average diameter of $300\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $20\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained (Figure 6(b)). Further, folic acid was applied as an alternative bioetcher to convert solid ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ into a hollow structure [80]. The obtained hollow ZIF-8(Zn) had an average thickness of $38~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , while the hollow cavity is characteristic with an average diameter of $168\mathrm{nm}$ .
Besides, the hollow cubic Ga-soc-MOF could be synthesized via using HCl solution as the etching agent and PVP as the surface protecting agent [51]. For example, highly monodispersed Ga-soc-MOF cubes with an average size around $300\mathrm{nm}$ were prepared via the solvothermal reaction between $\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -azobenzene tetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{4}A B T C\rangle}$ ligand in a mixed solution. Then, the HCl solution was added, and parts of the internal Gasoc-MOF cubes were etched, and mesoporous structures were prepared. With increasing the amount of HCl solution, the Ga-soc-MOF cubes could shrink considerably, and the well-defined hollow cubes with the average diameter of
$300\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $40\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained (Figure 6 (c)). It is noted that the etching degree of the Ga-soc-MOF cubes strongly correlated with the amount of HCl solution. Moreover, the surface of Ga-soc-MOF was protected by PVP, and the inside of the cubes was easily etched by the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ions with respect to the outside, thus leading to the formation of hollow structures.
Further, Liu et al. [69] synthesized the HoMOFs with mesopores inside of the shell via the selective etching method. In a typical synthesis, solid bimetallic MIL-101( $\mathrm{{Cr/}}$ Al) crystals were fabricated under hydrothermal conditions through the coordination between $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}/\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ . The synthesized MIL-101 $\mathrm{\langleCr/Al\rangle}$ crystals are characteristic with an average size of $200\mathrm{nm}$ and $\mathrm{Cr/Al}$ molar ratio of 1.75. The inner core of MIL-101( $\mathrm{{Cr/Al})}$ was less stable because it was formed through a boosted nucleation procedure. The hollow structure could be obtained by etching the inner core in hot acetic acid solution. Besides, the $\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ ions in the MIL-101 crystal were less stable than ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ ions, and they could also be removed by acetic acid solution, leading to mesoporous features in the shell. The obtained hollow MIL-101 $\mathrm{|Cr/Al)}$ with a $\mathrm{Cr/Al}$ molar ratio of 12.5 had an average cavity size of $170\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $30\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 6(d)).
In brief, using the selective etching strategy could directly produce the HoMOFs and preserve the initial morphology of solid MOFs. To date, there are still several problems during the etching processes, such as the uncontrolled location of the voids, the randomly distributed voids, simultaneously etched outside and inside of MOFs with ununiformed HoMOFs [63].
## (4) Ligand exchange method
The MOF-to-MOF transformation strategy has been developed to synthesize the HoMOFs through the ligand exchange approach [81], which involves two interweaving steps of dissolution and recrystallization. By precisely controlling the balance between the cleavage of old bonds and the establishment of new bonds, HoMOFs could be obtained.
Wu and co-workers [70] successfully fabricated hollow ZIF-8(Zn) cubes through the ligand exchange approach. MOF-5(Zn) nanocubes were synthesized by reacting $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ in DMF solution at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . To obtain the hollow ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ , MOF-5(Zn) nanocubes were added into the ethanol solution containing 2-MeIM at room temperature. The weaker $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ coordination in MOF- $5(\mathrm{Zn})$ could be dissociated and more stable $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ tetrahedral coordination was established in presence of 2-MeIM. With continuous ligand exchange, MOF-5(Zn) cubes dissolved and ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ shell started to nucleate on the outer of MOF$5(\mathrm{Zn})$ . Besides, as the ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ shell hindered the transport, 2-MeIM concentration of the inside was lower than outer shell. Therefore, the recrystallization of $Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ was slower than the dissolution of $\mathrm{MOF}{-}5(\mathrm{Zn})$ . The nonequivalent diffusion gave rise to the considerable voids near the interfaces of MOF-5/ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ . As the internal MOF-5 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ was completely disappeared, hollow ZIF-8(Zn) boxes with average length of $1.2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and shell thickness of $100\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained. Furthermore, this method could be expended to fabricate hollow $Z_{\mathrm{{IF}(Z n/C o)}}$ nanotube of by using M-BTC $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M=Co}$ , Zn) as the precursor. Similarly, Lu and coworkers [72] reported the transformation of monodisperse MOF-5(Zn) cubes into hollow $Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ spheres via the ligand exchange approach. In detail, MOF-5(Zn) cubes were synthesized by reacting $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}}}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ in a mixed solution containing ethanol, DMF and PVP. Then, MOF-5 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ cubes were added into a mixed solution of DMF and 2- MeIM at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Subsequently, the uniform hollow ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ spheres were obtained with the average diameter of $1.6\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and shell thickness of $236\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 6(e)).
Except for hollow ZIF-8(Zn), Xu and co-workers [71] reported the synthesis of hollow MOF-74(Co) via the solvothermal transformation of ZIF-67(Co) with the organic linker $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ . At the beginning, some tiny parts of the ZIF-67(Co) surface were corroded by the weak acid $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ . ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions dissolved and diffused into the region of solution close to the surface of ZIF-67(Co), and then, the bare ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions served as nucleation sites and reacted with DOBDC4− to form MOF-74(Co). As the reaction progressed, the outside of ZIF-67(Co) was surrounded by MOF-74(Co). As the relative amount of $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ was low, the core-shell structure was formed with an inside core of ZIF-67(Co) and outside shell of MOF-74(Co). With the increasing the amount of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ , the inside ZIF-67(Co) could be transformed completely and hollow single-shelled MOF-74 (Co) with the diameter of $300\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained (Figure 6(f)). Notably, ligand exchange method is expected as a powerful way to offer the unlimited possibilities for the design of various HoMOFs.
It is noted that template-free methods are broad classifications containing Ostwald ripening methods, competitive coordination methods selective etching methods and ligand exchange methods. The Ostwald ripening methods and competitive coordination methods could obtain HoMOFs in one-pot process, while the selective etching method and ligand exchange method need multiple steps to get HoMOFs. More template-free methods should be developed and extended to synthesize different kinds of HoMOFs.
Altogether, different single-shelled HoMOFs have been successfully prepared via hard-templating, soft-templating and template-free synthesis methods. However, it is still challengeable to achieve the precise preparation of HoMOFs and develop the general strategy to construct different HoMOFs due to their inherent advantages and disadvantages of each synthesis method (Table 1).
## 2.2 Synthesis of multi-shelled HoMOFs
Different from single-shelled HoMOFs, multi-shelled HoMOFs are a major type of burgeoning advanced structures, which have two or more shells with corresponding internal cavities and hollow cavity layers between each shell [82,83]. Multi-shelled HoMOFs have been widely recognized as one of the most promising functional materials. However, the precise fabrication of multi-shelled HoMOFs at the molecular level is still in its infancy.
Kim et al. [48] reported the first synthesis of doubleshelled hollow MIL-88A(Fe) via the emulsion-templating microfluidic system. In detail, the droplets containing the iron(III) in the aqueous solution were injected in a stream of organic phase containing the ligand (fumaric acid) and transported into the microreactor loop at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the continuous fabrication of the MOF spheres. Two pieces of Tjunctions were serially coupled in order to induce the formation of double emulsion droplets. While a single droplet was produced with a single T-junction as previously disclosed, a secondary T-junction was used to produce a second droplet around the first one. The final immiscible aqueous (inner)-organic (middle)-aqueous (outer) double emulsion droplets with two separate interfaces were transferred to the coil $30\mathrm{min}$ of residence time at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to induce the formation of double-shell hollow capsules with $310\upmu\mathrm{m}$ of inner shell and $440\upmu\mathrm{m}$ of outer shell. Besides, $\mathrm{{Xu}}$ and co-workers [71] fabricated the double-shelled MOF-74(Co) by using ZIF-67(Co) as precursor with ligand exchange strategy. The key factor involved is to control the concentration of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ , the ligand of MOF-74(Co). At the low concentration of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ , the diffusion rate is slower and only single-shelled hollow MOF-74(Co) could be obtained. With high concentration of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ , $\mathrm{H_{4}D O D B C}$ could diffuse through outer shell before the complete dissolution of ZIF-67(Co). After $\mathrm{H_{4}D O D B C}$ reacting with ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions at the internal space, the double-shelled MOF-74(Co) with the diameter of $300\mathrm{nm}$ and average shell thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ was formed.
Huo and co-workers [32] reported the synthesis of multishelled HoMOFs with the shell number up to 3 via the stepby-step crystal growth and selective-etching strategy. In detail, the as-prepared MIL-101(Cr) crystals are characteristic with a truncated octahedral shape and an average size of about $200\mathrm{nm}$ . When treated with aqueous acetic acid solution at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , the inner crystallites were preferentially etched, which enabled the formation of hollow cavities inside the MOF crystals. When using the initial MIL-101(Cr) particles as a template to react with additional chromium ions and aqueous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ solution for another $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , an additional layer of MIL-101(Cr) epitaxially grew, with the truncated octahedral particle size up to $350\mathrm{nm}$ . When these particles were immersed into an acetic acid aqueous solution for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , a double-shelled hollow MIL-101(Cr) was obtained. The average crystal size of double-shelled hollow MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ is $350\mathrm{nm}$ and the average thickness of three shell is $50\mathrm{nm}$ . Repeating the growth process three or more times with subsequent etching enabled the fabrication of tripleshelled hollow MIL-101(Cr) particles. The average crystal size of triple-shelled MIL-101(Cr) is $550\mathrm{nm}$ and the average thickness of three shell is $50\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 7(a–c)).
Besides, Tsung et al. [84] reported the synthesis of even quadruple-shelled hollow ZIF-8(Zn) via using the step-bystep crystal growth and selective-etching strategy. In the first step, ZIF-67(Co) nanocubes were synthesized by using aqueous $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and 2-MeIM assisted with CTAB. After that, ZIF-67(Co) nanocubes were added into the methanol solution containing 2-MeIM and $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}}}$ , and then the core-shell $Z\mathrm{IF-}67(\mathrm{Co})@Z\mathrm{IF-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ microcrystals were synthesized. To obtain double-shelled hollow $Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ , the $Z\mathrm{IF-}67(\mathrm{Co})\textcircled{\omega}Z\mathrm{IF-}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ was used as a core to epitaxially overgrow ZIF-8 $\mathbf{\nabla}Z\mathbf{n})$ . In the last step, the ZIF-67(Co) layers were dissociated in water and methanol solution with the volume ratio of 1 to form the hollow cavity layers between the ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ layers. Furthermore, to construct triple-shelled and quadruple-shelled ZIF-8(Zn), epitaxial growth of the ZIF-67(Co) and ZIF-8(Zn) would repeat three or four times. The average thickness of ZIF- $8(Z\mathfrak{n})$ shell is about $30\mathrm{nm}$ and the average thickness of hollow cavity between layers is about $40\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 7(d–f)).
In a word, it is very interesting to construct the multishelled HoMOFs via using emulsion droplets or MOF as templates or precursors. However, the formation process often involves complex growth procedures, various prerequisites and limited types of MOFs, thus leading to the construction of the fewer kinds of multi-shelled HoMOFs. Therefore, smart synthesis strategies are highly required to be developed for constructing diverse multi-shelled HoMOFs.
## 3 Synthesis of HoMOF based composites
To achieve multifunctional catalysis, it is of great importance to combine HoMOFs with various types of active components, such as metal NPs [39,85], metal oxides [86], POMs [87] and proteins [48], into a single counterpart, because they could integrate not only the function of individual components but also generate the novel and intriguing synergy properties. To date, different types of HoMOF based composites have been developed, and here we will focus on the encapsulation of active components by HoMOFs, and the obtained composites can be simply divided into three categories, including yolk-shell NP@MOF, NPs confined inside HoMOF shells and hollow satellite structures.
## 3.1 Synthesis of yolk-shell composites
Yolk-shell structure, which is also called “ship in bottle” structure, is one of the most common structures that combine the NP cores and microporous shells together. Typically, the active components are encapsulated inside the cavities of HoMOF structures or loaded on the inner surface of the HoMOF shell (Figure 8(a)).
Hard templates are often used to synthesize yolk-shell structures, such as metal oxide [39,85,88,89], $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ [9,38], PS spheres [90], and emulsion droplets [48,57]. In 2012, Tsung and co-workers [85] firstly reported the synthesis of yolkshell $\mathrm{Pd}\mathscr{@}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}})$ structures with edge sizes of octahedral Pd cores about $60\mathrm{nm}$ and the shell thickness of ZIF-8(Zn) around $100\mathrm{nm}$ via using $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as hard template. In a typical synthesis, Pd crystals were coated with $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to form coreshell $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ structures [91]. After that, the $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ structures were mixed with the precursors 2-MeIM and $Z\mathrm{n}$ $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ in methanol to grow the ZIF-8 $\mathbf{\mu}_{Z\mathbf{n}})$ shell. Because of the deprotonation of 2-MeIM during the formation of ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ , the $\mathsf{p H}$ value of the solution decreased from 7 to 5, and simultaneously, the generated protons could etch away $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to form the yolk-shell structures (Figure 8(b)). Besides, Huo and co-workers [39] synthesized the yolk-shell $\operatorname{Au}\ @\operatorname{H-}$ KUST- $1(\mathrm{Cu})$ structures with the average shell thickness of about $80\mathrm{nm}$ and diameter of cavity about $100\mathrm{nm}$ via using $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as templates and metal source for HKUST-1(Cu). In detail, core-shell $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ structures were first synthesized with Au cores of $13\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter, and then were added into the mixed solvents containing $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ , benzyl alcohol and ethanol at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight to obtain the yolk-shell structures. It is noted that the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ could not only provide ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions to grow HKUST-1 $\mathrm{(Cu)}$ shell but also protect the Au NPs against acid-induced corrosion or agglomeration (Figure 8(c)). In addition to $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ was also used as a hard template and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ source to construct the yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}\mathscr{@}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z{\mathrm{n}})$ structures [89]. Pd nanocubes with an average size of $26\mathrm{nm}$ were firstly synthesized in aqueous ascorbic acid solution with CTAB as surfactant. Subsequently, the as-prepared Pd nanocubes were used as the seeds for the surface nucleation of $Z\mathrm{nO}$ via in situ hydrolysis and condensation of the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions in the solution of methylenetetramine, CTAB and ascorbic acid at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After that, the $\mathrm{Pd}\ @Z\mathrm{nO}$ nanospheres were reacted with 2-MeIM to form the yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ structures with the shell thickness of $63~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and diameter of cavity $130\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 8 (d)), due to the strong coordination capability of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ atoms of 2-MeIM.
Cheng and co-workers [38] reported the synthesis of yolkshell $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ structures via using $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ as protective shell and hard template. In a typical process, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes with the average size of $140\mathrm{nm}$ were synthesized in an aqueous solution assisted with surfactant and ultrasound treatment. After that, the obtained $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes were coated with a layer of silica shell via the sol-gel method. Subsequently, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\textcircled{a}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was added into a methanolic solution containing $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}}}$ , 2-MeIM and PVP to grow ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ shell. Finally, the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ layer in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\ @\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\$ ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ was selectively etched with $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ to form yolkshell $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\ @Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z{\mathrm{n}})$ structures with the hollow cavity of $220\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 8(e)). After etching, the average size of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and yolk-shell $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{IF}{-}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ were well kept.
Besides, Liu and co-workers [92] synthesized the yolkshell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ structures via selective etching of UiO- $66(Z\mathrm{r}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and in situ reductions of Pd NPs in the cavity. In a typical synthesis, ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ was used to dig holes in UiO- ${\cdot66(\mathrm{Zr})}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and acetone was used as a protective agent for the external surface from etching. The obtained UiO- ${\cdot}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is characteristic of outer size of roughly $240\mathrm{nm}$ and internal cavity size of $108\mathrm{nm}$ . After that, the solution containing ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdCl}}_{4}$ and PVP was mixed with hollow UiO $\left.\phantom{-}66(Z\mathrm{r})-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ , while KBr and ascorbic acid were introduced to form the yolk-shell structures, in which cubic Pd core is characteristic with an average edge length of ${\sim}25.9\mathrm{nm}$ . No Pd NPs were observed on the external surface due to the large internal cavity of Ui ${\mathrm{O}}{-}66(\mathrm{}Z{\mathrm{r}}){-}{\mathrm{N}}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ to hold the Pd NPs and more defects of the shell for diffusing the ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdCl}}_{4}$ and ascorbic acid. In addition, Tsung and coworkers [40] reported the synthesis of multi yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ structures by directly etching the coreshell structure. Firstly, Pd NPs with an average size of $25\mathrm{nm}$ were added into the precursor of $\mathrm{\DeltaUiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , and multiple Pd NPs were encapsulated by $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ . Then, the samples were dispersed in peroxymonosulfate (PMS) solution and stirred at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The Pd NPs catalyzed PMS solution to produce reactive oxygen species for etching DOBDC in $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , and the obtained products are characteristic of multi yolk-shell structure and Pd NP located in isolated cavities with the average size of $100\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 8(f)). Furthermore, enzymes were successfully confined inside hollow ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ [93]. In detail, encapsulating enzymes by ZIF-67 (Co) were used as hard templates to overgrow ZIF-8(Zn) shells. Then, ZIF-67(Co) was etched in water, and yolk-shell enzymes ${\textcircled{a}}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z{\mathrm{n}})$ with an average shell thickness of $20\mathrm{nm}$ and cavity size of $60\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained.
In a word, several kinds of yolk-shell nanocomposites based on HoMOFs have been successfully developed via template-assisted and template-free methods. However, there are still some problems and challenges in the synthesis process, such as the complexity of the synthesis process, the precise control of the shell thickness and limited kinds of active species.
## 3.2 NPs confined inside HoMOF shells
## 3.2.1 Secondary components randomly distributed inside HoMOF shells
Active species in the micro-/meso-pores of HoMOF shell could prevent them from aggregation and achieve synergistic catalysis between them. To embed active species in the pores of HoMOFs, wet impregnation, selective etching or competitive coordination methods were developed.
Oh and co-workers [94] reported the confinement of bimetallic PdCo NPs in the micropores of hollow ZIF-67(Co). Firstly, core-shell $\mathrm{PS}@Z\mathrm{IF-}67(\mathrm{Co})$ was synthesized, and then it was added into the methanolic solution containing ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdCl}}_{4}$ to make $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ions diffuse into the ZIF-67(Co) shell. After that, PS template was removed and $\operatorname{Pd}^{2+}$ ions were reduced at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a nitrogen atmosphere. The formed hollow structure is characteristic with the average diameter of $1.33~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and shell thickness of $300\mathrm{nm}$ . Because the ZIF-67(Co) shells could be partly decomposed to release ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions, and then were reduced to form PdCo NPs with an average size of $2.7\mathrm{nm}$ . The lattice spacing of $0.220\mathrm{nm}$ corresponds to the (111) plane of PdCo NPs. Moreover, the PXRD pattern showed that the crystal structures of left ZIF67(Co) were well maintained.
Active species could participate in the formation of HoMOFs and coordinate with metal clusters or linkers of MOFs to form hollow composites. For example, Jiang and co-workers [10] reported the synthesis of $\mathrm{Au/ZIF{-}}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ nanocages via the selective etching method. The assynthesized ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ nanocubes with the average length of $130\mathrm{nm}$ were added into $\mathrm{NaAuCl}_{4}$ solution. As the ${\mathrm{Au}}^{3\bar{+}}$ ions exchanged with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions, the hollow $Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ structure was formed via the Kirkendall effect. When the outside diffusion of the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions through the shell is faster than the inside diffusion of the ${\mathrm{Au}}^{3+}$ ions, Kirkendall voids will be generated, eventually leading to the formation of a hollow nanocage. The synthesized $\mathrm{Au/ZIF{\cdot}8(Z n)}$ nanocages could maintain the crystal structure of $Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ and have a uniform shell thickness of $25\mathrm{nm}$ . The Au species were mainly in the form of a trivalent state and coordinated with two nitrogen atoms from two 2-MeIM molecules in ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ . In addition, Yu and co-workers [95] synthesized hollow $\mathrm{W/UiO}–66(Z\mathrm{r})$ nanocages via using $\mathrm{WO_{4}}^{2-}$ ions to selectively etch UiO- $66(Z\mathrm{r})$ . The as-prepared octahedral UiO-66 $(Z\mathrm{r})$ with the average diameter of $700\mathrm{nm}$ was added into the ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{WO}}_{4}$ solution by hydrothermal treatment at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Then, alkaline $\mathrm{WO_{4}}^{2-}$ could react with the acidic centers of ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ and etch the (100) faces of ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ , and the channels of $80\mathrm{nm}$ in width were formed. The $\mathrm{WO_{4}}^{2-}$ ions were bonded with hydroxy groups of $\left[Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\right]$ in the final hollow cage, and moreover, the elemental mapping of $\mathrm{W/UiO}–66(Z\mathrm{r})$ showed that $Z\mathrm{r}$ and W elements were welldistributed throughout the hollow cages.
Polyoxometalate (POM) could be introduced into HoMOFs via a competitive coordination strategy. The first integration of POM in HoMOFs was reported by Wang et al. [87]. The Keggin-type sodium phosphotungstate (NaPW) was added to the precursor of HKUST-1 $\mathrm{(Cu)}$ under the microwave treatment. The binding of cations and NaPW could promote the release of hydroxyl ion (OH ) generated by anion hydrolysis of metal salts. The emergence of $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ could accelerate the deprotonation of ligand, which quickly coordinated with metal ions. The final POM ions were encapsulated within the pores of HKUST- $1(\mathrm{Cu)}$ and bonded with $\mathrm{cu}$ sites of HKUST- ${\mathsf{I}}({\mathsf{C u}})$ . Moreover, when the molar ratio of NaPW to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions was increased up to 0.5, the HKUST- $1(\mathrm{Cu})$ was evolved into hollow spheres with an average diameter of $250\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $60\mathrm{nm}$ .
In a word, the secondary components could disperse inside HoMOF shells and strongly interact with the HoMOFs. However, the distribution of active components inside HoMOF shells is still difficult to control, and the active species are often dispersed inside or outside surfaces of HoMOFs.
3.2.2 Metal NPs sandwiched in HoMOF shells Generally, a sandwich structure is characteristic of two or
more layers, and each layer gets in touch with each other (Figure 9(a)). Hollow sandwich MOFs@NPs@MOFs composites could maximize the metal-support interface to regulate the activity, and the controllable shell prevents NPs from separating during the reaction process. Moreover, hollow sandwich structures have a larger space to absorb reactants and thin shells for improving the mass transfer and diffusion during the reaction process.
Zhang and co-workers [96] reported the synthesis of Pd NPs sandwiched in HoMOF shells. First, the sulfonated polystyrene spheres (SPS) were synthesized as the holding template to support the inner HKUST-1(Cu) shell, denoted as SPS $@$ HKUST-1(Cu). After that, Pd NPs were in situ reduced on the HKUST-1 $\mathrm{(Cu)}$ shell to obtain SPS@HKUST-1/ Pd structures. Subsequently, ZIF-8(Zn) was grown on the outer HKUST-1(Cu) shell by self-assembly to attain the SPS $@$ HKUST- $1(\mathrm{Cu})/\mathrm{Pd}\mathcal{@}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ . At last, the SPS template was removed by the dissolution of DMF and the final product, HKUST- $1(\mathrm{Cu})/\mathrm{Pd}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{ZIF}{\mathrm{-}}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ , was achieved. The average diameter of hollow spheres is about $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , while the outside shell thickness of the ZIF-8(Zn) is about $40\mathrm{nm}$ , and the inside shell thickness of HKUST-1(Cu) is about $20\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 9(b)).
Further, Zeng and co-workers [97] reported the synthesis of hollow sandwiched Ni NPs between hollow $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ and MOF-74(Ni) layer. First, hollow nickel silicate (NiSiO) spheres were synthesized via solid $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ spheres as both precursor and hard template. Solid $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ spheres were added into the aqueous solution containing sodium acetate and nickel sulfate under hydrothermal conditions. $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was etched by hydroxide ions released from dissolved sodium acetate and reacted with nickel ions to form nickel silicate thin platelets around the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ core. As the reaction proceeded, $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ phase was fully dissolved, and hollow $\mathrm{NiO}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ spheres were formed due to the Kirkendall effect. Further reductive transformation with hydrogen gas, $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ - supported Ni NPs were produced while preserving the hollow morphology. As Ni NPs could release $\mathrm{Ni}^{2\bar{+}}$ ions at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and react with the organic linkers $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ , MOF$74\mathrm{(Ni)}$ could nuclear and grow on the surface of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ spheres to form a hollow sandwich $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MOF}\ –74(\mathrm{Ni})$ structure. The mean diameter of the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ sphere is measured to be $260\mathrm{nm}$ while the shell thickness of MOF-74(Ni) is about $15\mathrm{nm}$ , and moreover, the sandwiched Ni NPs are around $4\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter (Figure 9(c)).
Besides, the magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs were introduced to synthesize the hollow sandwich $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z{\mathrm n})/$ $\mathrm{Pd}\mathscr{@}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z{\mathrm{n}})$ [98]. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs were supported on the PS nanospheres terminated with acrylic acid groups. Then, the ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ shell with a thickness of approximately $20\mathrm{nm}$ was grown to form $\mathrm{PS}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ structures. After that, via an incipient wetness impregnation method, Pd NPs were loaded on the ZIF-8(Zn) shell, and hence the $\mathrm{PS}(\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}.$ - $\mathrm{O}_{4}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})/\mathrm{Pd}$ nanospheres were formed. Subsequently, the ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ was further grown on the as-prepared samples, attaining the $\mathrm{PS}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@Z\mathrm{IF}\mathrm{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})/\mathrm{Pd}@Z\mathrm{IF}\mathrm{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ nanospheres. Finally, the PS nanospheres were removed by DMF solution, forming the hollow $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z{\mathrm{n}})/\mathrm{Pd}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8$ $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ nanospheres with the average size of Pd NPs about $2.29\mathrm{nm}$ and average diameter of the hollow structure about $520\mathrm{nm}$ . Moreover, the thickness of the inner shell was approximately $20\mathrm{nm}$ , while the thickness of the outer shell was approximately $35\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 9(d)).
In a word, the hollow sandwich structure could stabilize small NPs between layers of HoMOFs and utilize the interface between them for catalysis. However, the synthesis of hollow sandwich composite is still in its infancy and the cracks of two layers are hard to avoid.
## 3.3 Hollow satellite composites
The hollow satellite structures composed of solid core supported NPs and the outer shell are expected to provide an excellent option to achieve more functions compared with the yolk-shell structure (Figure 10(a)). The inner core or shell could support NPs and exhibit the interfacial effect between metal NPs and supports [99]. The outer shell could prevent the migration and aggregation of metal NPs [43]. Also, the hollow cavities could increase the mass transfer and diffusion of substrates and products [44].
Zhang and co-workers [100] reported the synthesis of hollow satellite 3D array nanoreactor via the hard-templating synthesis method. In a typical process, Rh NPs were loaded on the surface of $\mathrm{CLPS–SO_{3}H}$ , and $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ shells with an average thickness of $40\mathrm{nm}$ were coated as the templates. Then, the ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ was grown on the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ surface and the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ could be removed by $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ solution. Finally, the hollow satellite $\mathrm{IY-SO_{3}H/R h}@\mathrm{S-}Z\mathrm{IF-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ could be obtained. The hollow satellite structure contained sulfonated crosslinked polystyrene $(\mathrm{CLPS-SO_{3}H})$ core with an average size of $230\mathrm{nm}$ , Rh NPs satellite with an average diameter of $3.8\mathrm{nm}$ and cavity with a diameter of $300\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 10(b)). Zeng and co-workers [101] synthesized the hollow satellite Pt catalysts based on multi-shell hollow ZIF-67(Co) via using CTAB as a soft template. In a typical process, 2-MeIM, $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and CTAB were mixed at room temperature. CTAB could form micelles, which act as soft templates and nucleation sites for the formation of the first ZIF-67(Co) shell. After removal of the CTAB with ethanol, the singleshelled hollow ZIF-67(Co) with an average size of $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ could be obtained. When the growth-washing process was repeated for a second or third time with the as-prepared hollow ZIF-67(Co) as a seed, the double-shelled and tripleshelled ZIF-67(Co) could be successfully fabricated. Their overall diameters could increase up to $700\mathrm{nm}$ and $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Pt NPs were respectively deposited onto the multi-shell ZIF-67 (Co) supports via an in situ reduction method, in which $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ was used as a metal precursor and tetrabutylammonium borohydride was adopted as a mild organic reducing agent to avoid possible degradation of the MOF matrix during the metal deposition (Figure 10(c)). The average sizes of Pt NPs were $4.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the SSHMs/Pt, $3.4\mathrm{nm}$ for the DSHMs/Pt, and $2.6\mathrm{nm}$ for the TSHMs/Pt.
Li and co-workers [102] synthesized the hollow satellite structures, in which Pt NPs were supported on the core of yolk-shell $_{\mathrm{Ni/Zn-MOF}}$ . The yolk-shell ${\mathrm{Ni}}/{\mathrm{Zn}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ was synthesized via the self-assembly method. In detail, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were dissolved in a mixed solution containing DMF and ethylene glycol and then aged at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The microspheres with smooth surfaces were initially formed after $^{\textrm{1h}}$ , and further, these microspheres could partly resolve in the solution and the nanorodscomposed shells were grown on the surface of microspheres. Finally, a yolk-shell structure was formed at $6\mathrm{{h}}$ due to the Kirkendall effect. In another way, the 2 nm Pt NPs stabilized by PVP were added into the solution containing the MOF microspheres and aged at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $4.5\mathrm{h}$ to obtain the hollow satellite structures. The obtained hollow satellite $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{Ni}/Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF was characteristic with the average diameter of about $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , the outer shell thickness of about $250\mathrm{nm}$ and the inner core diameter of about $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure 10 (d)).
In a word, the hollow satellite structure could combine the advantages of the yolk-shell structure and support structure. However, the complexity of the synthesis process and the difficulty in precisely controlling the satellite structure limited the further development of hollow satellite structures.
## 4 Thermal catalysis
HoMOFs based nanocatalysts provide great opportunities to achieve heterogeneous catalysis because of the rich metal nodes, abundant organic ligands, tunable porous structures, the large voids and the short pathway of HoMOFs [38]. For pristine HoMOFs, the metal nodes and functional ligands can be used as active sites for heterogeneous catalyses, such as Lewis acid catalysis, Lewis base catalysis, and synergistic catalysis of both [103]. Beyond those, the tunable pore structures of HoMOFs can achieve molecular sieving and confinement catalysis [85]. Furthermore, active components like metal NPs, metal oxides and POMs are integrated with HoMOFs for different catalytic reaction systems and even for multifunctional catalysis [14].
## 4.1 HoMOFs as active species
To date, HoMOF itself can be directly used as a heterogeneous catalyst and the reported catalytic active sites can be divided into two categories: (1) the coordinatively unsaturated metal nodes as active sites, and (2) the intrinsic organic ligands or post-modification ligands as active sites.
The coordinatively unsaturated metal nodes could act as the active sites for organic catalytic reactions. For example,
Liu et al. [69] reported the synthesis of hollow bimetallic MIL-101 $\mathrm{{Cr/Al})}$ with the average diameter of hollow cavities about $230\mathrm{nm}$ , and the average shell thickness of about $30\mathrm{nm}$ via selective etching method. The atomic ratio of Cr to Al in the solid MIL-101 $\mathrm{(Cr/Al)}$ is 1.75, while it was increased up to 12.5 in the hollow MIL- $101(\mathrm{Cr/Al})$ . Further, it possesses the BET surface area of $2812.5\mathrm{m}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ and corresponding mesopore size of $3.8\mathrm{nm}$ calculated by the BarrettJoyner-Halenda (BJH) method. When used as a catalyst for oxidation of 4-chlorostyrene using tertbutyl hydroperoxide (TBHP) as the oxidant, the hollow MIL-101 $\mathrm{(Cr/Al)}$ showed $80\%$ conversion and $60\%$ selectivity of benzaldehyde, higher than $30\%$ conversion and $5\%$ selectivity for the solid counterpart. Due to the hollow mesoporous shell with smaller diffusion blockage, the mass transfer and diffusion of substrates and products could be significantly enhanced, and thus, minimize the overoxidation. Besides, Huo and coworkers [32] fabricated the truncated octahedral multishelled hollow MIL-101(Cr) for styrene oxidation reaction. When used as catalysts for styrene oxidation reaction in the presence of tert-butyl hydroperoxide (TBHP) at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ (Figure 11(a)), the conversions are about $18\%$ for solid MIL-101(Cr), $60\%$ for single-shelled MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , $75\%$ for double-shelled MIL-101(Cr), and $90\%$ for triple-shelled MIL-101(Cr), while the selectivity of benzaldehyde are $25\%$ , $40\%$ , $50\%$ , and $55\%$ , respectively (Figure 11(b)). Besides, there were slight changes in terms of the catalytic activity and selectivity after five cycles (Figure 11(c)). The enhanced catalytic performance of triple-shelled MIL-101(Cr) could be ascribed to the enhanced substrate adsorption of hollow interiors between each layer. In addition, Peng et al. [53] reported the metal clusters as Lewis acid sites in the hollow $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC for cycloaddition of propylene oxide (PO) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to produce propylene carbonate (PC). In the absence of catalysts, the yield of PC is only $20.1\%$ , but the yield of PC is enhanced significantly via using the hollow $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC as catalysts, and the yield of PC is $97.4\%$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ . Moreover, no deactivation was found over the hollow ZnBTC during the successive three recycle tests.
Nitrogen-containing ligands of HoMOFs could act as basic sites for heterogeneous catalysis. For example, Sun and coworkers [104] reported the hollow bimetallic ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Ni})$ as catalysts for Knoevenagel condensation reaction. By adjusting the ratio of $\mathrm{Zn/Ni}$ salts, different shaped samples are obtained, such as the butterfly, hollow butterfly and hollow ring ZIF-8. The molar ratios of Ni to $Z\mathrm{n}$ were 1.0:99.0 for the butterfly, 3.7:96.3 for the hollow butterfly, and 9.2:90.8 for ring particles, and moreover, Ni atoms were well dispersed in the whole sample. When used as catalysts for Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehyde and malononitrile at room temperature for $2\mathrm{h}$ , the conversion of benzaldehyde is $38.5\%$ for solid sphere, $47.2\%$ for butterfly, $51.6\%$ for hollow butterfly and $68.8\%$ for hollow ring ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Ni})$ . It is noted that the catalytic activity of ring ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Ni})$ is nearly two times of solid spherical ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Ni})$ . Doping $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions could form micropores of $1.1\mathrm{nm}$ to improve the transport of substrates and increase the amount of basicity. To further understand the enhanced performance, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -TPD spectra were performed to test the basicity of ZIF$8(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Ni})$ . The basicity of the hollow ring shape is 6.7 (unit basicity)/g with respect to 3.1 (unit basicity)/g for the solid sphere. The diffusion rate and the amount of basicity of hollow ring ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Ni})$ could both contribute to the enhanced catalytic activity. In addition, Li et al. [11] reported the ZIF-8(Zn) with 3D ordered cavity as a catalyst for Knoevenagel condensation reaction with respect to polycrystal hollow ZIF-8(Zn) and bulk ZIF-8(Zn). The ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ with 3D ordered cavity is characteristic with a cavity size of $270\mathrm{nm}$ and $1.3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in particle size, while polycrystal hollow ZIF-8(Zn) had cavity size of $250\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $40\mathrm{nm}$ . When used as catalysts for Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehyde and malononitrile, the complete conversion time is $2\mathrm{h}$ for ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ with 3D ordered cavity with respect to $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ for polycrystal hollow ZIF-8(Zn) and $8\mathrm{h}$ for bulk ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ . The enhanced activity could attribute to the facile accessibility of reactants to active sites of ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ with 3D ordered cavity, while the macropores of polycrystal hollow ZIF-8(Zn) were completely occluded in the microporous matrix and thus were much less accessible to reactants. Moreover, ZIF-8(Zn) with 3D ordered cavity showed higher stability and it could be reused at least seven times. The benzaldehyde conversion is decreased from $94.6\%$ to $87.0\%$ during the recycling test compared with that from $70\%$ to $20\%$ for polycrystal hollow ZIF-8(Zn). The higher stability can be attributed to its robust singlecrystalline framework with much lower defect density in comparison with the polycrystal hollow ZIF-8(Zn). Further, Li and co-workers [105] reported the hollow ZIF-8(Zn) nanospheres as a catalyst for $[3+3]$ cycloaddition reactions of 1,3-cyclohexanedione and 3-methyl-2-butenal along with solid ZIF-8(Zn). Hollow ZIF-8(Zn) nanospheres displayed the $89\%$ conversion and $99.9\%$ selectivity of 2,2-dimethyl2,6,7,8-tetrahydro-5H-chromen-5-one with respect to $73.2\%$ conversion and $99.9\%$ selectivity for solid ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ and $15.9\%$ conversion and $85.5\%$ selectivity for mixture of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and 2-MeIM. It is noted that the synergistic acidbase effect of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and imidazolate in the hollow ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ could make two substrates closely and produce the valuable pyranyl heterocycles.
In a word, the unsaturated metal nodes and organic ligands of HoMOFs, together with the porous structures, could be applied for catalytic reactions. The short diffusion could promote the enhanced catalytic performances. Therefore, imparting HoMOFs with more functions could be applied for diverse catalytic systems.
## 4.2 HoMOF based composites for catalysis
4.2.1 Encapsulating metal NPs by HoMOFs for catalysis Metal NPs are typical active components for many catalytic reaction systems [89]. Compared with traditionally supported metal catalysts, HoMOF based metal nanocatalysts have been recognized as the emerging materials for catalysis. Among various composites, encapsulating the metal NPs with HoMOFs could exhibit some potential advantages over the solid counterparts [106], such as molecular sieving catalysis, selective chemical catalysis, and multifunctional catalysis, along with high stability.
(1) Encapsulating metal NPs by HoMOFs for molecular sieving catalysis
Tsung and co-workers [85] reported the first example of yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ for shape-selective hydrogenation of different model molecules, including ethylene, cyclohexene, and cyclooctene with respect to the supported $\mathrm{Pd}/$ ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ . Both samples are characteristic with $60\mathrm{nm\Pd}$ NPs as active sites. As for ethylene hydrogenation, the catalytic activities are $3.2{\times}10^{-3}\ \mathrm{mol\g_{\mathrm{Pd}}^{-}\Sigma_{\mathrm{}}^{-1}\Sigma_{\mathrm{}}^{-1}}$ for yolk-shell Pd@ZIF-8(Zn) and 3.0 × 10−3 mol gPd−1 s−1 for Pd/ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ . These indicated that similar ethylene hydrogenation kinetics occurred, and there was no significant diffusional influence between the two samples. However, as for the cyclooctene hydrogenation, no activity was found over yolkshell catalysts, but the $\mathrm{Pd/ZIF{-}}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ catalyst showed good activity. It is noted that the size of cyclooctene $(5.5\mathrm{\AA})$ is larger than the pore size of ZIF-8( $\mathrm{\DeltaZn)}$ (3.4 Å), thus leading to the difficulty in diffusion through the ZIF-8(Zn) shell to contact with Pd NPs. Moreover, Huo and co-workers [39] encapsulated dendritic $\mathrm{Pt}$ -on-Au nanostructures within the hollow $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC as catalysts for the liquid-phase hydrogenation of $n$ -hexene and cis-stilbene. The size of PtAu NPs is about $100\mathrm{nm}$ and the average thickness of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC is
$80\mathrm{nm}$ . Two olefins could both be completely converted by bare PtAu NPs. However, the conversions were $25\%$ for $n$ - hexene and zeroed for cis-stilbene over the yolk-shell PtAu $\mathsf{N P s@C u}$ -BTC. Owing to the larger molecular size of cisstilbene $(5.3{\times}9.8\mathrm{\AA}$ ), it is difficult to diffuse through the pore apertures of the HKUST- $1(\mathrm{Cu)}$ shell $(9\mathrm{\AA})$ to the inside PtAu NPs.
Different from single metal NP in yolk-shell structures as active sites, multi yolks in one MOF crystal were fabricated to further enhance the catalytic performance. For example, Su et al. [9] synthesized multi-yolk-shell $\operatorname{Au}@Z\operatorname{IF-8}(Z\mathfrak{n})$ nanoreactors for catalytic aerobic oxidation of alcohols along with core-shell $\operatorname{Au}\ @Z\operatorname{IF}-8(Z\mathrm{n})$ . When added into the methanolic solution containing 1-butanol, 1-hexanol, and 3- phenylpropanol at the reaction temperature of $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , respectively, the conversions are $50.0\%$ for 1-butanol, $40.2\%$ for 1-hexanol and zero for 3-phenylpropanol. It is noted that 3-phenylpropanol is big enough $(6.3{\times}11.9\mathrm{\AA}$ ) with respect to the pore aperture $(3.4\textup{\AA})$ of ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ shell, thus leading to the difficulty in the catalytic reaction. As a contrast, the coreshell $\operatorname{Au}\mathcal{@}Z\operatorname{IF-8}(Z\mathfrak{n})$ nanostructure delivers a much lower conversion of 1-butanol $(17.3\%)$ and 1-hexanol $(15.7\%)$ than those of multi-yolk-shell $\operatorname{Au}(\mathcal{Q}Z\operatorname{IF}-8(Z\operatorname{n})$ nanoreactor. The large void of $\operatorname{Au}\ @Z\operatorname{IF-8}(Z\mathfrak{n})$ nanoreactor can significantly facilitate the mass transfer of reactants and products by reducing the diffusion length and provide sufficient space for maximizing the active sites accessible to alcohol molecules. In addition, Tsung and co-workers [40] synthesized multi yolk-shelled $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ for catalytic hydrogenations of cyclohexene $(4.2\mathrm{\AA})$ and cyclooctene $(5.5\mathrm{\AA})$ at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The conversions are $97\%$ for cyclohexene and zero for cyclooctene, due to the size of substrate larger than about $4\textup{\AA}$ for the aperture size of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (Figure 12(a)). Moreover, multi yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ showed the higher activity for 1-octylene $(80\%)$ and allylbenzene $(80\%)$ hydrogenation than $55\%$ for yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ . As for hydrogenation of 1-octylene and allylbenzene, multiyolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ displayed $80\%$ conversion for both substrates, while $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ showed the conversions of $56.5\%$ for 1-octylene and $50.7\%$ for allylbenzene (Figure 12(b)). These results indicated that multi-yolk-shell architecture is more effective for increasing the diffusion kinetics during the catalytic reactions.
(2) Synergy catalysis between metal NPs and HoMOFs
HoMOFs have some functions like substrate enrichment and regulation of electronic structures of active sites for achieving heterogeneous catalysis. For example, Liu and coworkers [92] synthesized the yolk-shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for hydrogenation of 1-chloro-2-nitrobenzene into 2-chloroaniline with respect to Pd cubes and supported catalysts including $\mathrm{Pd/UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , Pd/hollow UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ and Pd cubes/UiO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ . When supported catalysts are used, direct dehalogenation occurred, and they are $19.3\%$ for pure Pd cubes, $2.4\%$ for $\mathrm{Pd/UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $1.3\%$ Pd/hollow UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $3.6\%$ for Pd cubes/UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ . As a contrast, when Pd NPs encapsulated by HoMOFs were used, no nitrobenzene was detected, suggesting that the byproduct of nitrobenzene can be inhibited by the position of Pd in the composites. The high selectivity of chloroaniline could be ascribed to the competitive chemisorption between hydrogen and reactant molecules on the surface of Pd atoms. Compared with Pd NPs on the outside, the encapsulation structure can slow down the direct chemisorption rate of reactant around Pd, thus leading to direct dehalogenation instead of hydrogenation. Besides, different selectivity of 2- chloroaniline could be obtained over different shaped Pd encapsulated by hollow $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . They are $83.6\%$ for cubes, $87.6\%$ for rods, $82.3\%$ for truncated octahedrons, $86.5\%$ for icosahedrons, $87.1\%$ for concave cubes and $6.4\%$ for small particles. The regular shapes possessed much more reaction active sites (terrace or edge) for hydrogenation, while the small particle or aggregated structures preferred the dehalogenation.
Further, Li et al. [107] synthesized $10\mathrm{nm\Pd}$ NPs encapsulated within hollow $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{IF}(Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{n}/C_{ Ḋ }0 Ḍ )$ for CO oxidation and acetylene hydrogenation along with Pd NPs supported by solid ZIF $\scriptstyle(Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/C_{0})$ . For the CO oxidation, the hollow $\operatorname{Pd}\ @Z\operatorname{IF}$ $\scriptstyle(Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/C_{0})$ displayed the complete conversion of CO at a lower temperature of $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with respect to $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for solid ZIF $\scriptstyle(Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/C_{0})$ supported Pd NPs. As for acetylene hydrogenation, hollow $\mathrm{Pd}\ @Z\mathrm{IF}(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co})$ showed the $80\%$ conversion of acetylene and $80\%$ selectivity of ethylene with respect to $590\%$ conversion of acetylene but low ethylene selectivity $(<30\%)$ for bare $\mathrm{Pd}\ N\mathrm{Ps}/\mathrm{C}$ (Figure $12({\mathrm{c}},{\mathrm{d}}){\mathrm{~}}$ ). These could be ascribed to the synergistic effect of hollow $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{IF}(Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{n}/C_{ Ḋ }0) Ḍ$ and Pd NPs. Moreover, Pd NPs are the active species for CO oxidation and acetylene hydrogenation, and the hollow interior of $Z_{\mathrm{{IF}}}(Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/C_{0})$ enhanced the gas storage to enhance the activity. Besides, the hollow interior of $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{IF}(Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{n}/C_{ Ḋ }0 Ḍ )$ might enable the increased inward diffusion rate of acetylene and the preferential desorption of ethylene from the Pd surface, which would be beneficial for the efficient and selective semi-hydrogenation of acetylene.
Li and co-workers [102] constructed the hollow $\mathrm{Pt}\ @\mathrm{Ni}/$ Zn-BDC with the molar ratio of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ of 1 via the selfassembly method. Four kinds of composites including Pt NPs located in the center of yolk-shell ${\mathrm{Ni}}/{\mathrm{Zn}}{\mathrm{-MOF}}$ $\mathbb{P}\mathrm{t_{in}}(\mathcal{Q})$ MOF(Y)] and satellite structure $\mathrm{Pt}_{\mathrm{void}}@\mathrm{MOF}(\mathrm{Y})$ as well as Pt NPs located in the center of solid ${\mathrm{Ni}}/{\mathrm{Zn}}{\mathrm{-MOF}}$ $[\mathrm{Pt}\ @\mathrm{MOF}$ (S)] and on the outer surface of ${\mathrm{Ni}}/{\mathrm{Zn}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ $\mathrm{[Pt/MOF(S)]}$ were tested for selective hydrogenation of $^{\alpha,\beta}$ -unsaturated cinnamaldehyde (Figure 13(a–c)). Satellite structure $\mathrm{Pt}_{\mathrm{void}}(\mathcal{Q})$ MOF(Y) showed the high conversion $(97\%)$ and high selectivity $(98.2\%)$ toward cinnamyl alcohol for $24\mathrm{h}$ under $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 MPa $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Under the same condition, the conversions are $56\%$ for $\mathrm{Pt/MOF(S)}$ , $77\%$ for $\mathrm{Pt}\ @\mathrm{MOF}(\mathrm{S})$ , $70\%$ for $\mathrm{Pt_{in}}@\mathrm{MOF(Y)}$ , and correspondingly the selectivities of cinnamyl alcohol are $85\%$ , $90.8\%$ , and $94.2\%$ . The high conversion was ascribed to the short diffusion distance of the reactants from the MOF surface to the $\mathrm{Pt}\mathrm{NPs}$ , and moreover, Pt NPs were well stabilized by satellite structure. Besides, the enhanced catalytic selectivity of cinnamyl alcohol was ascribed to the synergistic effect between Pt and $\mathrm{{Ni}}/Z\mathrm{{n}}$ -MOF, which could be beneficial for adsorption and activation of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bond over the conjugated $C{=}C$ bond (Figure 13(d)).
## 4.2.2 Encapsulating metal compounds with HoMOFs for catalysis
Except for metal NPs, other metal compounds, including metal oxides, hydroxides, and sulfides, are combined with HoMOFs for catalysis. Compared with metal NPs encapsulated within HoMOFs, the research on encapsulating metastable metal compounds with HoMOFs is still limited.
Cai et al. [86] reported the synthesis of yolk-shell $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{\bar{3}+}.$ -BDC nanoreactors for catalytic degradation of 4-chlorophenol (4-CP) in the presence of peroxymonosulfate (PMS). The yolk-shell $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ were octahedral shape with an average size of $240\mathrm{nm}$ and shell thickness of $15\mathrm{nm}$ , while the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs were nearly spherical shape and an approximate diameter of $60\mathrm{nm}$ . When used as catalysts, the degradation rate of 4-CP significantly increased in the presence of yolk-shell $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\$ MOFs catalysts and PMS, and almost $100\%$ decomposition was achieved within $60\mathrm{min}$ , while only $59.6\%$ decomposition was observed for bare $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{NPs}$ and $50\%$ for core-shell $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ under the same condition. The high catalytic activity could be ascribed to the following two reasons. First, the shell thickness and high porous structure promoted the rapid diffusion of PMS and 4-CP. Second, the yolk-shell structure could promote the adsorption of 4-CP because of the hollow cavities and $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction between 4-CP and organic ligands of MOF shell (Figure 14(a)).
Wang et al. [108] synthesized yolk-shell copper hydroxysulfates $(\mathrm{CHS})@\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC for catalytic aldehyde acetalization reactions (Figure 14(b)). The CHS with a size of $7{\-}9\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and the inner core of ${\sim}5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ was encapsulated by $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC with a shell thickness of $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . For the aldehyde acetalization with ethanol, a high yield of diethyl acetals $(88\%)$ was obtained by using $\mathrm{CHS}@\mathrm{Cu-BTC}$ as a catalyst. When CHS and pure Cu-BTC were used as the catalysts, the yields of diethyl acetals were $80\%$ and $17\%$ , respectively. It is noted that the CHS core could promote the acetalization of benzaldehyde, while the Cu-BTC shell as the reactor provides multiple transportation channels for the reactants to access the active CHS surface. The reactants are restricted in a limited space between the core and shell of $\mathrm{CHS}@\mathrm{Cu-}$ BTC, thus accelerating the catalytic reaction rate. Stability tests indicated that the catalytic performance of $\mathrm{CHS}@\mathrm{Cu-}$ BTC is well maintained after being recycled more than five times. On the other hand, the CHS catalyst showed that the conversion of benzaldehyde dramatically decreased to $28\%$ .
Wang et al. [109] synthesized sodium phosphotungstate (Keggin-type POM, abbreviated as $\mathrm{{NaPW}}_{12})$ incorporated into the $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ -BTC nanotubes as a catalyst for aerobic oxidation of thiophenol to diphenyldisulfide. The asprepared $\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{B}^{\prime}$ TC nanotubes had the average diameter of $108~\mathrm{nm}$ and wall thickness of $48~\mathrm{nm}$ , while the $\mathrm{{NaPW}}_{12}$ was well dispersed in the shell of nanorods. As for catalytic oxidation of thiophenol into diphenyldisulfide, the
POM@Fe/Co-BTC nanotubes showed the $92.5\%$ yield of diphenyldisulfide, higher than $80\%$ for $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ -BTC or $35\%$ for POM. The synergistic effect between $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ -BTC and POM could promote catalytic performance. Besides, the catalyst showed good reusability, and the yield of $87.5\%$ was observed at the fifth cycle, only slightly lower than $92.5\%$ for the first cycle.
Jiang and co-workers [110] synthesized double-shell layered Co double hydroxides, Co-LDH@ZIF-8(Zn), via hard-templating methods. The hollow LDH (H-LDH) with the shell thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ acted as templates to epitaxially grow ZIF-8 shell with a thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ , and the finial sample, hollow $\mathrm{Co-LDH}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{ZIF-}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ , was characteristic with the average size of $800\mathrm{nm}$ . Besides, metalloporphyrin, such as Co-centered porphyrin (Co-TCPP), as an example of functional guest, can be encapsulated in the hollow interior of $\mathrm{H-LDH}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ to afford $\mathrm{Co-TCPP}@\mathrm{H-LDH}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8$ $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ (Figure 14(c)). For catalytic cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with epibromohydrin, the $\mathrm{Co-TCPP}@\mathrm{H-LDH}@Z\mathrm{IF-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ exhibited the $94\%$ conversion of epibromohydrin and $599\%$ selectivity of cycloaddition product cyclic carbonates within $48\mathrm{{h}}$ under 1 atm $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and room temperature (Figure 14(d)), significantly higher than $64\%$ yield for the solid composite of Co-TCPP in situ embedded in ZIF- $8(Z\mathrm{n})$ (denoted Co$\mathrm{TCPP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8(Z{\mathrm{n}}))$ and $88\%$ yield for free Co-TCPP. In addition, the $\mathrm{Co-TCPP}@\mathrm{H-LDH}@Z\mathrm{IF-}8(Z\mathrm{n})$ showed good recyclability during the successive three runs.
In a word, encapsulating secondary components by HoMOFs could exhibit excellent catalytic performances with respect to individual components. However, the types of secondary components are still in their infancy and the synergistic effect of HoMOFs with other components still needs to be studied on more catalytic reactions.
## 5 Summary and prospects
We have summarized the recent progress on the synthesis of various HoMOFs and their composites with other kinds of active components as well as their applications in thermal catalysis. To date, various kinds of HoMOFs with different shells have been successfully fabricated via hard-templating, soft-templating and template-free synthesis methods (Table 2). Besides, the secondary components, including metal NPs, metal oxides, metal hydroxides and POMs have been integrated with HoMOFs to construct the yolk-shell, hollow sandwich, and hollow satellite structures. Furthermore, HoMOFs based catalysts could exhibit the enhanced catalytic performances in different catalytic systems with respect to the solid counterparts.
Although much progress has been made, the study on HoMOFs is still at the early stage and some problems need to be solved as follows: (1) The synthesis strategies of HoMOFs are still in their infancy. There are lots of challenges in the precise synthesis and regulation of HoMOFs in the desired shape, size, and shell thickness. Besides, the cracks or breakage of the shell cannot be ignored for HoMOFs, which will negatively influence the catalytic performances. (2) Compared with pristine HoMOFs, the study of HoMOF based composites is still in its infancy. The secondary components are mainly limited to metal NPs, and more types of active species should be integrated with HoMOFs. (3) The relationships among the composition, structure and catalytic performances are still not clear. Besides, the synergistic effect between secondary components and HoMOFs needs to be further studied.
To address the above, we give some pieces of advice for future researches about HoMOFs based catalysts as follows. (1) It is important to explore more synthesis strategies to economically, precisely and large-scale prepare HoMOFs based catalysts. Besides, new technologies like in situ characterization and machine learning could be introduced to probe and predict the synthetic procedure and the formation mechanism of HoMOFs. (2) The interactions among different components, metal nodes and organic ligands of HoMOFs should be studied in heterogeneous catalysis, and thus novel composites should be developed. For example, other typical components, including two-dimensional materials, zeolites, nanoclusters and even single sites, could be integrated with HoMOFs to construct the target nanocatalysts. Further, covalent organic frameworks (COFs) as emerging porous materials have been attracted a large amount of attention. The combination of HoMOFs with
COFs could produce novel composites with core-shell or sandwich structures as high-performance catalysts [111– 114]. (3) HoMOFs provide an opportunity to investigate the structure-function relationship in heterogeneous catalysis relating to their tunable shell thickness, hollow cavities and intrinsic micro-/meso-pores. The in situ techniques should be developed for monitoring the reaction behaviors during the reaction processes and better understanding the reaction mechanism.
In summary, the HoMOFs have been recognized as emerging materials and possess great potential to achieve high-performance catalysis with respect to their solid counterparts. We hope that this review will bring the readers with new insights to better synthesize the well-defined HoMOFs based catalysts and understand the catalytic reaction mechanism.
